.TH NSH 1 "2026-11-15" "NSH 0.2.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nsh \- Neo SH. Just Vanilla SH.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nsh
.RB [ \-iln ]
.RB [ \-\-posix | \-\-vanilla ]
.RB [ \-\-profile " name" ]
.RB [ \-\- ]
.RI [ file " [" arguments ...]]
.br
.B nsh
.RB [ \-iln ]
.B \-c
.I command
.RI [ name " [" arguments ...]]
.br
.B nsh
.RB [ \-iln ]
.B \-s
.RI [ arguments ...]
.br
.B nsh
.B \-\-set-default
.RB [ \-\-dry-run ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
NSH interprets shell programs on Linux and provides an interactive terminal
editor. The tested POSIX.1-2024 behavior and known gaps are documented in
.I /usr/share/doc/nsh/POSIX.md .
.PP
An explicit
.B nsh script.sh
reads the file with the NSH interpreter. When directly executing a file, the
kernel uses its interpreter line if present. A
.B #!/bin/sh
script uses the system shell. A
.B #!/usr/bin/env nsh
script uses the NSH found in PATH.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-c
Execute the following argument as shell input. The next argument supplies
.BR $0 ;
subsequent arguments are positional parameters.
.TP
.B \-i
Request interactive behavior.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-login
Request login startup. Invocation with a leading hyphen in the program name
also selects this mode.
.TP
.B \-n
Parse commands without running them.
.TP
.B \-s
Read commands from standard input.
.TP
.B \-\-posix
Select the POSIX shell interface and its interactive ENV behavior. See the
release notes for the verified scope.
.TP
.B \-\-vanilla
Skip startup files and use in-memory history. This mode can start a session
when configuration contains errors.
.TP
.BI \-\-profile " name"
Select an interactive profile for this invocation.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Show usage.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the NSH version.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The interactive configuration source is
.IR ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/nsh/conf.sh .
It is interpreted once at session startup. Commands entered through
.B nsh set
change session memory. The configurable editor, history, profiles, modules,
hooks, settings and trust rules are described in
.I /usr/share/doc/nsh/CONFIG.md .
.PP
The editor uses arrow keys for history and leaves Tab unbound by default.
The history file is
.IR ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-$HOME/.local/state}/nsh/history .
.SH LOGIN SHELL
.B nsh \-\-set-default \-\-dry-run
displays the current and proposed shells, prerequisite checks and the
rollback command. Run it from the installed
.IR /usr/bin/nsh .
.PP
.B nsh \-\-set-default
requires confirmation on a foreground controlling terminal before invoking
.BR chsh (1).
Keep the working session open, save the rollback command and test the new
login in a separate session. See
.I /usr/share/doc/nsh/MANUALS/LOGIN.md
for registration, rollback and removal.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Ordinary execution returns the status of the last command, or the value
supplied to
.BR exit .
A command missing from PATH returns 127. A command found but unavailable for
execution returns 126.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sh (1),
.BR chsh (1),
.BR shells (5),
.BR pacman (8),
.BR dpkg (1),
.BR rpm (8)
.SH LUA PLUGINS
.TP
.B \-\-plugins
Load saved plugins for this invocation, including when running a script.
.TP
.B \-\-no-plugins
Skip loading Lua plugins. This option also takes precedence over
.BR \-\-plugins .
.PP
.B nsh plugin list
lists available plugins.
.B nsh plugin enable NAME
changes the active set inside the session. An external invocation saves the
choice for future sessions. Use
.B command nsh
or an absolute path to invoke the executable from NSH.
See
.I /usr/share/doc/nsh/PLUGINS.md
and
.I /usr/share/doc/nsh/PLUGIN_API.md .
